amdxdna: reuse shape-compatible PARTIAL_ELF single commands

`iree_hal_amdxdna_find_single_command_cache_descriptor_template_entry` keys the
single-dispatch cache on the *identity* of the executable-owned run template
(`src_asm_inst`/`src_patches` pointers). A model that fans one HAL dispatch out
over many distinct templates therefore never hits: qwen3.6-moe:35b-a3b issues
~13.7k single dispatches per 1k-token prefill across its expert layers and
misses 13591 of them, with `src_asm_inst` distinct every time. Each miss
allocates a control-code BO, maps a GPU VA, locks it, makes it resident, and
destroys the whole thing again one dispatch later.

The allocation itself is not the expensive part. Every `MakeResident` records a
paging fence, and `SubmitCommandToHwQueueAfterPaging` drains all pending paging
fences before *each* hardware-queue submit -- of which this path issues ~3.3 per
dispatch (the dispatch, its opcode-9 code publication, and the code-range
release). 13.6k fresh allocations therefore tax 44.8k submits, and the wait
ahead of those submits dominates the prefill.

Nothing about the rewrite needs template identity.
`iree_hal_amdxdna_rewrite_cached_single_partial_elf_cmd` re-patches every
control word from the *fresh* descriptor, rebinds every buffer, and refreshes
the entry's host-side shadow copies; the cached descriptor is never read as a
source of truth. All it requires is a compatible shape: same queue, same CU
index, equal control-word count (the BO was sized for it) and equal binding
count.

So look up an idle PARTIAL_ELF entry by that shape when identity misses, and
rewrite it in place. Because the rewrite realizes a *different* descriptor into
the entry, the entry's recorded identity is updated to match, or a later
identity lookup would match contents that are no longer there -- the same
staleness class that 0005 fixed for the chain cache. Capacity stays at 8: the
shape matcher reuses entries instead of accumulating them, so a larger cache
measured no better (hit rate is already ~100% after the first few dispatches).

56b572e8c ("amdxdna: reduce Windows Path-B dispatch synchronization") already
did exactly this for the chain cache ("Reuse compatible PARTIAL_ELF chain
entries before allocating new cache entries"); the single-dispatch path that MoE
takes was left out.

Measured on Windows Strix Halo (1022:17f0), driver 32.0.203.314, FastFlowLM 1k
bench, qwen3.6-moe:35b-a3b, on top of 0006:

| metric                                | before | after |
|---------------------------------------|--------|-------|
| prefill tok/s                         |  38.65 | 44.84 |
| TTFT s                                |  25.27 | 21.78 |
| pending-paging wait before submits    |  5.1s  | <0.2s |
| control-code BO allocations           | 13591  |    ~8 |

Hardware-queue submit count is unchanged (44847); only the paging wait ahead of
them goes away. Decode is unaffected (14.4 tok/s either way) because decode
issues few dispatches per token.

Apply on top of: bb10dfb81 ("amdxdna: treat Windows UMDRIVERPRIVATE as adapter
info"), with 0004-0006 applied.

Tested: Windows Strix Halo (1022:17f0), driver 32.0.203.314. `dev.py
test-fastflowlm` smoke PASS with coherent multi-turn output; qwen3.6-moe:35b-a3b
1k, qwen3.5:0.8b 1k+4k, qwen3:0.6b 1k, gemma4-it:e2b 1k all PASS with zero `ert
state 8` timeouts.
